WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.660
Pointer safety includes knowing how much memory is valid to access.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:10.360
A pointer to the first element of a sequence does not, by itself, describe how many elements are available.

3
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:17.660
That is why pointer-plus-length APIs, spans, containers, and range-based operations are so important.

4
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.600
They keep access and bounds information close enough for callers and reviewers to reason about them together.

5
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.840
Prefer standard containers and views where possible.

6
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.460
std::array expresses fixed-size storage.

7
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.640
std::vector and std::string track dynamic size.

8
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.220
std::span can represent a bounded non-owning view when the source data outlives the span.

9
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:45.720
Iterators and algorithms can reduce manual pointer arithmetic and make the valid range more visible.

10
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:52.940
These tools do not remove every logic error, but they reduce how often a raw address must carry hidden size information.

11
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.900
Raw pointer arithmetic deserves careful review.

12
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:04.920
Offsets, counts, signed and unsigned conversions, and multiplication used for allocation can all create invalid ranges.

13
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:14.060
Before copying, parsing, transforming, or indexing, validate that the requested range fits the available data and the destination.

14
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.480
Treat null termination assumptions carefully.

15
00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:24.660
A buffer with a length is not necessarily a C string, and a C string may not be appropriate when embedded nulls or explicit lengths matter.

16
00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.900
The defensive habit is to preserve length with data.

17
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.360
If an API accepts a pointer, ask where the valid range comes from, who checked it, and whether the pointer and length can diverge.

18
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:43.700
When ranges are derived from untrusted input, check the arithmetic before forming the pointer or view, not only after dereference.

19
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.800
Invalid intermediate pointers can already be undefined behavior.

20
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:54.780
Good designs make out-of-range access harder to express and easier to catch in tests and review.
